        "subject": "LU-15248 tests: cap racer overstripe count relative to OSTCOUNT",
        "message": "LU-15248 tests: cap racer overstripe count relative to OSTCOUNT\n\nfile_create.sh picks a random overstripe count in [0, RACER_LOV_MAX_STRIPECOUNT]\nfor every file, and defaulted RACER_LOV_MAX_STRIPECOUNT to the filesystem-wide\nLOV_MAX_STRIPE_COUNT (2000).  That bound does not scale with the number of OSTs:\non a small config such as racer@ldiskfs+DNE with 2 OSTs it makes about half of\nall files hugely overstriped -- hundreds to ~2000 stripes, i.e. hundreds of\nstripes per OST.\n\nEvery whole-file operation on such a file -- truncate, a time/owner setattr\n(both must reach every OST object), or a write spanning stripes -- builds a\nclient cl_io lockset with one extent sub-lock per stripe.  lov enqueues them\nsequentially and synchronously and each granted sub-lock stays pinned until the\nwhole io finishes, so the operation runs for O(stripes) RPC round-trips.  Under\nracer's concurrency that sweep outlasts the server's ~100s lock-callback\ntimeout, and either the operation's own held sub-locks or a concurrent op\nholding one of them (e.g. a write blocked on the object's truncate semaphore\nbehind the slow setattr) is evicted -- a data-loss event that also repeats and\nblows the test's time budget.  Crash-confirmed on frozen-at-eviction vmcores:\nexpiring PW extent locks were sub-locks hundreds deep into 600-1900-stripe\ntruncate/setattr locksets.\n\nCap the default to RACER_MAX_OVERSTRIPE_PER_OST (16) times OSTCOUNT, clamped to\nLOV_MAX_STRIPE_COUNT.  This still exercises overstriping heavily -- many stripes\nper OST -- without the pathological tail, and both knobs remain overridable for\nlarge-OST-count testing.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nTest-Parameters: optional testlist=racer\nSigned-off-by: Oleg Drokin <green@whamcloud.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:Opus-4.8 llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: I6868a16354d555bd5cacd0042cca84b5a9afd687\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
